Secretions (Death Rattle)
Palliative & End of Life
Loud wet respiratory crackles, Pooling oropharyngeal secretions, Absent cough/swallow reflex

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PEL-045
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe psychological distress of family members due to loud, wet, "rattling" breathing in a deeply unconscious, actively dying patient.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, adult daughter, son
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported the family was in a state of extreme panic and distress, believing the deeply unconscious patient was actively suffocating or drowning due to loud, bubbling respiratory secretions that failed to clear with repositioning. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to definitively confirm the terminal phase, provide intense psychological first aid to the family, and administer parenteral anticholinergics to halt the secretion production and relieve the acoustic distress prior to the morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Daughter states: "He is drowning in his own fluid. It sounds horrific, every breath is bubbling, and we can't get it out. Please help him, he is suffocating."
History of Present Illness: The patient is in the terminal dying phase and has been completely unconscious for 24 hours. Over the last 4 hours, there has been an accumulation of thick, tenacious salivary and bronchial secretions explicitly pooling deeply within the oropharynx. This has resulted in loud, coarse, and highly distressing wet respiratory crackles distinctly audible without a stethoscope from across the room. There is a profound loss of the normal cough or functional swallow reflex strictly secondary to severe terminal bulbar weakness. The occurrence is specifically isolated strictly to the final hours or days of the terminal dying phase. Crucially, there is an absolute lack of overt respiratory distress or subjective dyspnea strictly from the patient's own perspective (no grimacing, no accessory muscle use). The distinct bubbling sound is continuously synchronized explicitly with both the inspiratory and expiratory tidal cycles. There is profound, severe psychological distress explicitly experienced strictly by the bedside family members. The patient exhibits total, unarousable unresponsiveness, effectively rendering the patient completely unaware of the accumulated fluid.
Functional Status: Unarousable coma; actively dying.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe family psychological trauma; misguided attempts by family to aggressively suction deep in the throat risking mucosal injury and bleeding.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 80/40 mmHg (estimated, weak radial) | HR: 110 bpm | Temp: 36.5 C | RR: 22 bpm (shallow) | SpO2: 88% on room air | Wt: N/A
General: Patient is completely unarousable, relaxed facial expression, taking shallow breaths with extremely loud, transmitted upper airway bubbling sounds.
Physical Exam: The explicit clinical diagnosis is strictly made visually and acoustically at the bedside, absolutely requiring no further diagnostic or explicitly invasive laboratory testing. Observation confirms the patient is deeply comatose without any signs of central respiratory distress (no nasal flaring, no tracheal tug, no brow furrowing). Auscultation confirms the loud crackles are entirely transmitted from the upper airway (oropharynx), not the deep lung bases. The diagnostic tetrad is fulfilled by the loud wet respiratory crackles, the obvious pooling oropharyngeal secretions, and the clinically absent cough/swallow reflex.
Environmental Observations: The family has a loud suction machine running and has been attempting to use rigid catheters in the patient's mouth, causing minor bleeding to the gums.
Medication Reconciliation: The patient is on a baseline continuous subcutaneous infusion of morphine and midazolam, ensuring deep comfort.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Secretions (Death Rattle) - Status: Exacerbated (Terminal Phase / Family Distress)
Differentials:

Acute Pulmonary Edema (Left Ventricular Failure) - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary cause of the noise, as the sounds are clearly upper airway (gurgling/rattling) rather than deep, fine basal crackles, and the patient lacks the acute physical distress or gasping associated with active pulmonary edema.

Pneumonia - Ruled out/unlikely to be the actionable driver at this stage; while infection may be present, the terminal lack of swallow reflex is the mechanical cause of the pooling, and antibiotics are futile in the actively dying phase.

Foreign body airway obstruction - Ruled out/unlikely due to the continuous tidal airflow and bilateral chest rise, alongside the characteristic acoustic profile of pooled fluid.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is in the final, actively dying phase, exhibiting classical terminal secretions ("death rattle"). The objective clinical assessment definitively confirms the patient is deeply comatose and completely free of any subjective physical distress or air hunger, despite the horrific noise. The crisis is entirely secondary to the profound psychological trauma experienced by the family, who misinterpret the noise as active suffocation. Because the family is highly distressed and attempting dangerous deep suctioning, delaying assessment until standard hours would result in severe, lasting psychological trauma for the family and potential physical injury to the patient. Immediate after-hours intervention was absolutely necessary to provide definitive clinical reassurance, cease the traumatic suctioning, and administer stat subcutaneous anticholinergics to dry the airway.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered stat subcutaneous hyoscine butylbromide (an anticholinergic) to rapidly halt further production of salivary and respiratory secretions. Gently instructed the family to immediately cease all deep oral suctioning. Repositioned the patient slightly to the side to allow gravity to assist with secretion drainage.
Positive Behavioral Change: (Directed at Family) When you hear the loud rattling noise (trigger), explicitly look directly at your father's relaxed forehead and chest rather than listening to the sound (behavior). This visual confirmation proves he is not grimacing or fighting for air, definitively reassuring you that the noise is merely fluid moving in a relaxed throat, completely preventing your panic (positive consequence).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Continued community palliative care nursing support.
DME/Safety (Optional): Discontinue use of the mechanical suction machine. Use only soft sponge swabs for the lips.
Follow-up: The patient is actively dying; standard routine GP follow-up is no longer applicable. Bereavement support to follow.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the patient develops visible physical distress, such as severe grimacing, thrashing, or the breathing completely stops, the family is instructed to administer the prescribed subcutaneous comfort medications and contact the palliative care triage line to confirm the passing. Do not call 000.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The anticholinergic medications will stop new fluids from forming, but they cannot dry up the fluid that is already pooled there. The noise may soften but might not disappear completely.
Clinical Course: The patient is in the final hours to days of life. This is a natural, expected part of the deep physiological relaxation that occurs before death.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Family: Do not attempt to use the suction machine down the throat. Do not attempt to give any fluids or water by mouth. Focus entirely on maintaining a calm, loving presence.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: (To Family) I know the sound is absolutely terrifying, but I can assure you clinically that he is in a deep coma and cannot feel or hear it. Because his swallow reflex has relaxed, normal saliva is simply vibrating as the air passes over it. It is like snoring—loud to us, but the person sleeping feels nothing. The injection I gave will stop the body from making more saliva, and we have turned his head to let it drain.
Actionable Steps: Put the suction machine away. Turn on soft music to help mask the sound in the room. Hold his hand and speak softly to him.

